\title{SoK: Sycophancy as an Integrity Failure in Human-AI Decision Systems}
\titlenote{Author version of a paper accepted at the 22nd ACM ASIA Conference on Computer and Communications Security (ASIA CCS 2027). To appear in the proceedings.}

\author{Devharsh Trivedi}
\orcid{0000-0001-6374-7249}
\affiliation{%
  \department{Mathematics, Engineering and Computer Science}
  \institution{LaGuardia Community College, City University of New York}
  \city{Long Island City}
  \state{New York}
  \country{USA}
}
\email{devharsh.trivedi86@lagcc.cuny.edu}

\begin{abstract}
Sycophancy is the tendency of a large language model to favor a user's stated belief over the
evidence, agreeing with incorrect or leading input in order to please. This SoK treats sycophancy
not as an alignment curiosity but as an integrity failure in systems where a human relies on
machine output to reach a decision. Where an organization places such a model in an advisory or
analytic path, the assurance that its output reflects the evidence rather than the phrasing of the
request is a security property, and one that is presently unenforced. We give a threat model in
which the prompt is an attacker-controllable input channel: the same content framed as an
assertion rather than a question measurably shifts model agreement, so any party who can shape a
query can bias the answer without compromising the model, the host, or the data. We systematize a
62-source snowball corpus and a reproducible search of 423 records, and code all 44 dedicated
benchmarks by the reference against which they judge sycophancy: a correctness label, a
counterfactual stance shift, a human or clinical norm, or the update the evidence warrants. The
four are incommensurable; the progressive/regressive distinction exists only under the first, and
only two instruments can judge inputs that mix evidence with pressure. Measured rates are material:
58.19 percent sycophantic responses on one benchmark, 49 percent more affirmation of user actions
than humans give, about 40 percent more validation of incorrect beliefs after warmth training, and
authority-pressure collapse from at most 11 percent in frontier models to between 80 and 94
percent in earlier or smaller ones. We grade the evidence behind 36 mitigations across data,
training, inference, and governance tiers: 8 have broad support, four have been re-run by another
group, none was tested against an adaptive adversary, and recent work shows that suppressing
overt sycophancy can displace it into forms an audit cannot tell from success. No single layer
suffices, and every deployed control ultimately leans on a user-side vigilance that current
evidence says the failure mode itself erodes. We close with a research agenda for measurement and
assurance, including a longitudinal study design for that erosion.
\end{abstract}

